{# Ventilation advisor — should you open a window? Pure decision math; entity reads (states/now)
   stay in the caller (bedroom_window_advisor, files/automations.yaml). Returns 'stale' (indoor air
   is stale, ventilate), 'cool' (free cooling available), or 'none' (don't suggest). Every non-'none'
   verdict REQUIRES outdoor air to be under the safe caps (pm_safe / pm10_safe) — so the system can
   never advise ventilating into unsafe air (the smoke guard). Args coerce inside (like lighting.jinja)
   so a caller may pass strings or numbers. Unavailable -> safe 'none'.
   Unit-tested in tests/test_ventilation_macros.py. Tune the comfort band / cool delta / PM cap here.
   'stale' outranks 'cool' when both apply. #}
{%- macro ventilation_advice(indoor_temp, outdoor_temp, indoor_pm, outdoor_pm, air_stale,
                             outdoor_pm10=0, comfort_lo=55, comfort_hi=78, cool_delta=5,
                             pm_safe=25, pm10_safe=50) -%}
{%- set it = indoor_temp | float(0) -%}
{%- set ot = outdoor_temp | float(999) -%}
{%- set ip = indoor_pm | float(0) -%}
{%- set op = outdoor_pm | float(999) -%}
{%- set op10 = outdoor_pm10 | float(999) -%}
{%- set stale = air_stale | bool -%}
{# Smoke guard: block on fine PM2.5 over its cap OR coarse PM10 over its own (higher) cap — the two
   absolute air-quality gates. There is deliberately NO relative "outdoor dirtier than indoors" term:
   a HEPA purifier drives indoor PM near zero, so any such term vetoed CO2/cooling ventilation on
   normal safe-but-moderate outdoor air (e.g. outdoor 18 vs a scrubbed indoor 2) — the recurring
   regression this macro kept re-introducing across three fixes (a relative margin, then a relative
   floor, both leaky). Objectively-safe outdoor air (under the caps) is fine to bring in; the purifier
   re-scrubs it. Genuinely bad air is caught by the absolute pm_safe / pm10_safe caps alone. The
   indoor_pm arg is retained for call-site compatibility but no longer gates. #}
{%- if op > pm_safe or op10 > pm10_safe -%}none
{%- elif stale and comfort_lo <= ot <= comfort_hi -%}stale
{%- elif it > comfort_hi and (it - ot) >= cool_delta -%}cool
{%- else -%}none
{%- endif -%}
{%- endmacro -%}

{# The CLOSE direction — the mirror of ventilation_advice, added when the SNZB-04 contact sensor
   made "is the window actually open?" knowable (2026-08-25). Same split: pure decision math here,
   entity reads stay in the caller (bedroom_window_close_advisor, files/automations.yaml). Returns
   'smoke' (outdoor air went over the caps), 'rain' (weather turned wet), 'cold' (the room is being
   chilled) or 'none'.
   The caller MUST gate on the window actually being open. This macro takes no contact-state
   argument by design — the same split as every other macro here — so on its own it would happily
   advise closing a window that is already shut.
   Precedence is smoke > rain > cold: all three are reasons to close, so the most urgent one wins
   the message. The PM caps default to ventilation_advice's, so a window opened on advice is told
   to close on the same thresholds it was opened under — keep the two in step when tuning.
   Unit-tested in tests/test_ventilation_macros.py. #}
{%- macro close_window_advice(indoor_temp, outdoor_temp, outdoor_pm, raining,
                              outdoor_pm10=0, comfort_lo=55, indoor_floor=68,
                              pm_safe=25, pm10_safe=50) -%}
{# Unavailable readings default SAFE — toward 'none' — so a dead sensor cannot nag. That is the
   OPPOSITE default from ventilation_advice above (which defaults outdoor temp and PM to 999),
   and deliberately so: there the unsafe direction is telling you to open up, here it is telling
   you to close. Each macro defaults toward silence, not toward its own verdict. #}
{%- set it = indoor_temp | float(999) -%}
{%- set ot = outdoor_temp | float(999) -%}
{%- set op = outdoor_pm | float(0) -%}
{%- set op10 = outdoor_pm10 | float(0) -%}
{%- set wet = raining | bool -%}
{%- if op > pm_safe or op10 > pm10_safe -%}smoke
{%- elif wet -%}rain
{# Cold needs BOTH terms. Outdoor below the comfort floor alone is not enough — an open window on a
   45°F night with the room still at 72°F is doing exactly what it was opened for. The room has to
   have actually dropped below indoor_floor before this nags. #}
{%- elif ot < comfort_lo and it < indoor_floor -%}cold
{%- else -%}none
{%- endif -%}
{%- endmacro -%}
